ud6.5:11.1 | P: Santi paneke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:11.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are not eternal, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: (none)
ud6.5:12.1 | P: Santeke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:12.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are both eternal and not eternal, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: (none)
ud6.5:13.1 | P: Santi paneke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:13.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are neither eternal nor not eternal, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: (none)
ud6.5:14.1 | P: Santeke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:14.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are made by oneself, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: “Pleasure & pain, the self & the cosmos are self-made” …
ud6.5:15.1 | P: Santi paneke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:15.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are made by another, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: “other-made” …
ud6.5:16.1 | P: Santeke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:16.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain are made by both oneself and another, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: “both self-made & other-made” …
ud6.5:17.1 | P: Santi paneke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:17.2 | S: “Pleasure and pain have arisen anomalously, not made by oneself or another, and the self and the cosmos.” | T: “Pleasure & pain, the self & the cosmos–without self-making, without other-making–are spontaneously arisen. Only this is true; anything otherwise is worthless.”
ud6.5:18.1 | S: They were fighting, quarreling, and disputing, continually wounding each other with barbed words: | T: And they kept on arguing, quarreling, & disputing, wounding one another with weapons of the mouth, saying,
ud6.5:18.2 | S: “Such is Truth, such is not Truth! | T: “The Dhamma is like this, it’s not like that.
ud6.5:18.3 | S: Such is not Truth, such is Truth!” | T: The Dhamma’s not like that, it’s like this.”
ud6.5:19.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks adjusted their under robes and–carrying their bowls & robes–went into Sāvatthī for alms.
ud6.5:19.2 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what was happening. | T: Having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud6.5:19.3 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinnā kho te bhikkhū bhagavantaṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One,
ud6.5:20.1 | P: “Idha, bhante, sambahulā nānātitthiyasamaṇabrāhmaṇaparibbājakā sāvatthiyaṁ paṭivasanti nānādiṭṭhikā nānākhantikā nānārucikā nānādiṭṭhinissayanissitā. | T: “Lord, there are many contemplatives, brahmans, & wanderers of various sects living around Sāvatthī with differing views, differing opinions, differing beliefs, dependent for support on their differing views….
ud6.5:21.1 | P: Santeke samaṇabrāhmaṇā evaṁvādino evaṁdiṭṭhino: | T: (none)
ud6.5:21.2 | P: ‘sassato attā ca loko ca, idameva saccaṁ moghamaññan’ti …pe… | T: (none)
ud6.5:21.3 | P: te bhaṇḍanajātā kalahajātā vivādāpannā aññamaññaṁ mukhasattīhi vitudantā viharanti: | T: And they keep on arguing, quarreling, & disputing, wounding one another with weapons of the mouth, saying,
ud6.5:21.4 | P: ‘ediso dhammo, nediso dhammo; | T: ‘The Dhamma is like this, it’s not like that.
ud6.5:21.5 | P: nediso dhammo, ediso dhammo’”ti. | T: The Dhamma’s not like that, it’s like this.’”
ud6.5:22.1 | P: “Aññatitthiyā, bhikkhave, paribbājakā andhā acakkhukā; | T: “Monks, the wanderers of other sects are blind & eyeless.
ud6.5:22.2 | P: atthaṁ na jānanti anatthaṁ na jānanti, dhammaṁ na jānanti adhammaṁ na jānanti. | T: They don’t know what is beneficial and what is harmful. They don’t know what is the Dhamma and what is non-Dhamma.
ud6.5:22.3 | P: Te atthaṁ ajānantā anatthaṁ ajānantā, dhammaṁ ajānantā adhammaṁ ajānantā bhaṇḍanajātā kalahajātā vivādāpannā aññamaññaṁ mukhasattīhi vitudantā viharanti: | T: Not knowing what is beneficial and what is harmful, not knowing what is Dhamma and what is non-Dhamma, they keep on arguing, quarreling, & disputing, wounding one another with weapons of the mouth, saying,
ud6.5:22.4 | P: ‘ediso dhammo, nediso dhammo; | T: ‘The Dhamma is like this, it’s not like that.
ud6.5:22.5 | P: nediso dhammo, ediso dhammo’”ti. | T: The Dhamma’s not like that, it’s like this.’”
ud6.5:23.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud6.5:24.1 | S: “Some ascetics and brahmins, it seems, | T: (none)
ud6.5:24.2 | S: cling to these things. | T: With regard to these things they’re attached– some contemplatives & brahmans.
ud6.5:24.3 | S: They founder in mid-stream, | T: Not reaching the footing, they sink in mid-stream.
ud6.5:24.4 | S: without reaching that objective.” | T: (none)
ud6.5:24.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti95
iti95:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti95:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three ways of being provided with sensual pleasures. | T: “Monks, there are these three ways of obtaining sensual pleasures.
iti95:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti95:2.3 | S: Some sensual pleasures are simply present; some are for those who love to create; and some are for those who control what is created by others. | T: Those whose sensual pleasures are already provided, those who delight in creating, those with control over what is created by others.
iti95:2.4 | S: These are the three ways of being provided with sensual pleasures.” | T: These are the three ways of obtaining sensual pleasures.”
iti95:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti95:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti95:3.1 | S: “Sensual pleasures that are simply present, | T: Devas whose pleasures are already provided,
iti95:3.2 | S: gods who control what is created by others, | T: those with control,
iti95:3.3 | S: gods who love to create, | T: those who delight in creation,
iti95:3.4 | S: and others who indulge in sensual pleasures— | T: and any others enjoying sensual pleasures
iti95:3.5 | S: They go from this state to another, | T: in this state here or anywhere else,
iti95:3.6 | S: but don’t escape transmigration. | T: don’t go beyond the wandering-on.
iti95:4.1 | S: Knowing this danger | T: Knowing this drawback
iti95:4.2 | S: in sensual indulgence, an astute person | T: in sensual pleasures, the wise
iti95:4.3 | S: would forsake all sensual pleasures, | T: should abandon all sensual desires,
iti95:4.4 | S: both human and heavenly. | T: whether human or divine.
iti95:5.1 | S: Having cut the stream so hard to pass, | T: Having cut the flow of greed for lovely, alluring forms so hard to transcend,
iti95:5.2 | S: that’s tied to pleasant seeming things, | T: (none)
iti95:5.3 | S: they become fully extinguished, | T: having, without remainder, comprehended stress, they are, without remainder, totally unbound.
iti95:5.4 | S: completely transcending suffering. | T: (none)
iti95:6.1 | S: Seers of the noble truths, knowledge masters, | T: They, the wise, with an attainer-of-wisdom’s noble vision,
iti95:6.2 | S: the astute, understanding rightly, | T: right gnosis,
iti95:6.3 | S: directly know the ending of rebirth, | T: directly knowing the ending of birth,
iti95:6.4 | S: they come not back to future lives.” | T: come to no further becoming.
iti95:7.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti95:7.2 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti111
iti111:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti111:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, live by the ethical precepts and the monastic code. Live restrained in the monastic code, conducting yourselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, keep the rules you’ve undertaken. | T: “Be consummate in virtue, monks, and consummate in the Pāṭimokkha. Dwell restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in your behavior & sphere of activity. Train yourselves, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
iti111:3.1 | S: When you’ve done this, what more is there to do? | T: “When one is consummate in virtue, consummate in the Pāṭimokkha; dwelling restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in one’s behavior & sphere of activity; training oneself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults–what more is to be done?
iti111:4.1 | S: Suppose a mendicant has got rid of desire and ill will while walking, and has given up dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. Their energy is roused up and unflagging, their mindfulness is established and lucid, their body is tranquil and undisturbed, and their mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: “If, while he is walking, any greed in a monk is done away with, any ill will… any sloth & torpor… any restlessness & anxiety is done away with, any uncertainty is abandoned; if his persistence is aroused and not lax; if his mindfulness is established & unmuddled; if his body is calm & unaroused; if his mind is centered & unified:
iti111:4.2 | S: Such a mendicant is said to be ‘keen and prudent, always energetic and resolute’ when walking. | T: then a monk walking with such ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously resolute, one with persistence aroused.
iti111:5.1 | S: Suppose a mendicant has got rid of desire and ill will while standing … | T: “If, while he is standing.…
iti111:5.2 | P: Ṭhitopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto ātāpī ottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ āraddhavīriyo pahitattoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti111:6.1 | S: sitting … | T: “If, while he is sitting.…
iti111:6.2 | P: Nisinnopi, bhikkhave, bhikkhu evaṁbhūto ātāpī ottāpī satataṁ samitaṁ āraddhavīriyo pahitattoti vuccati. | T: (none)
iti111:7.1 | S: or when lying down while awake. | T: “If, while he is lying down, any greed in a monk is done away with, any ill will… any sloth & torpor… any restlessness & anxiety is done away with, any uncertainty is abandoned; if his persistence is aroused and not lax; if his mindfulness is established & unmuddled; if his body is calm & unaroused; if his mind is centered & unified:
iti111:7.2 | S: Such a mendicant is said to be ‘keen and prudent, always energetic and resolute’ when lying down while awake.” | T: then a monk lying down with such ardency & compunction is called continually & continuously resolute, one with persistence aroused.”
iti111:7.3 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti111:7.4 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti111:8.1 | S: “Carefully walking, carefully standing, | T: Controlled in walking, controlled in standing,
iti111:8.2 | S: carefully sitting, carefully lying; | T: controlled in sitting, controlled in lying down,
iti111:8.3 | S: a mendicant carefully bends their limbs, | T: controlled in flexing & extending his limbs
iti111:8.4 | S: and carefully extends them. | T: (none)
iti111:9.1 | S: Above, below, all round, | T: –above, around, & below,
iti111:9.2 | S: as far as the planet extends; | T: as far as the worlds extend–
iti111:9.3 | S: they scrutinize the rise and fall | T: observing the arising & passing away
iti111:9.4 | S: of phenomena such as the aggregates. | T: of phenomena, of aggregates:
iti111:10.1 | S: Meditating diligently like this, | T: a monk who dwells thus ardently,
iti111:10.2 | S: peaceful, not restless, | T: not restlessly, at peace–
iti111:10.3 | S: training in what leads to serenity of heart, | T: always mindful, training in the mastery of awareness-tranquility–
iti111:10.4 | S: always staying mindful; | T: (none)
iti111:10.5 | S: they call that sort of mendicant | T: is said to be continually
iti111:10.6 | S: ‘always determined’.” | T: resolute.
iti111:11.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti111:11.2 | P: Dvādasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti20
iti20:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti20:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, when I’ve comprehended the mind of an individual whose mind is corrupted, I understand: | T: “Monks, there is the case where a certain person is corrupt-minded. Having encompassed that mind with [my] awareness, I discern,
iti20:2.2 | S: ‘If this individual were to die right now, they would be placed in hell as if delivered there.’ | T: ‘If this person were to die at this instant, then as if he were to be carried off, he would thus be placed in hell.’
iti20:2.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
iti20:2.4 | S: Because their mind is corrupted. | T: Because his mind is corrupt.
iti20:2.5 | S: Corruption of mind is the reason why some sentient beings, when their body breaks up, after death, are reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell.” | T: It’s because of corrupt-mindedness that there are cases where beings–at the break-up of the body, after death–reappear in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, hell.”
iti20:2.6 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti20:2.7 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti20:3.1 | S: “Knowing an individual’s | T: Knowing the case
iti20:3.2 | S: corrupted mind, | T: of a corrupt-minded person,
iti20:3.3 | S: the Buddha explained this matter | T: the One Awakened explained its meaning
iti20:3.4 | S: in the mendicants’ presence. | T: in the presence of the monks.
iti20:4.1 | S: If that individual | T: If that person
iti20:4.2 | S: were to die at this time, | T: were to die at this instant,
iti20:4.3 | S: they’d be reborn in hell, | T: he’d reappear in hell
iti20:4.4 | S: for their mind is corrupted. | T: because his mind is corrupt–
iti20:5.1 | S: That sort is cast down as surely | T: (none)
iti20:5.2 | S: as if they’d been carried off and put there. | T: as if he were carried off and placed there.
iti20:5.3 | S: For corruption of mind is the reason | T: It’s because of corrupt-mindedness
iti20:5.4 | S: sentient beings go to a bad place.” | T: that beings go to a bad destination.
iti20:6.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti20:6.2 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
iti20:6.3 | P: Dutiyo vaggo. | T: (none)
iti20:7.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
iti20:7.1 | P: Moho kodho atha makkho, | T: (none)
iti20:7.2 | P: Vijjā taṇhā sekhaduve ca; | T: (none)
iti20:7.3 | P: Bhedo sāmaggipuggalo, | T: (none)
iti20:7.4 | P: Vaggamāhu dutiyanti vuccatīti. | T: (none)

## an7.36
an7.36:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, you should associate with a friend who has seven factors. | T: “Monks, a friend endowed with seven qualities is worth associating with.
an7.36:1.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
an7.36:1.3 | S: They give what is hard to give. They do what is hard to do. They endure what is hard to endure. They reveal their secrets to you. They keep your secrets. They don’t abandon you in times of trouble. They don’t look down on you in times of loss. | T: He gives what is hard to give. He does what is hard to do. He endures what is hard to endure. He reveals his secrets to you. He keeps your secrets. When misfortunes strike, he doesn’t abandon you. When you’re down & out, he doesn’t look down on you.
an7.36:1.4 | S: You should associate with a friend who has these seven factors. | T: A friend endowed with these seven qualities is worth associating with.
an7.36:2.1 | S: A friend gives what is hard to give, | T: “He gives what is beautiful, hard to give;
an7.36:2.2 | S: and does what’s hard to do. | T: does what is hard to do;
an7.36:2.3 | S: They put up with your harsh words, | T: endures painful, ill-spoken words.
an7.36:2.4 | S: and with things hard to endure. | T: (none)
an7.36:3.1 | S: They tell you their secrets, | T: His secrets he tells you;
an7.36:3.2 | S: and keep your secrets for you. | T: your secrets he keeps.
an7.36:3.3 | S: They don’t abandon you in times of trouble, | T: When misfortunes strike, he doesn’t abandon you;
an7.36:3.4 | S: or look down on you in times of loss. | T: when you’re down & out, doesn’t look down on you.
an7.36:4.1 | S: The individual in whom | T: (none)
an7.36:4.2 | S: these things are found is your friend. | T: A person in whom these traits are found, is a friend to be cultivated
an7.36:4.3 | S: If you want to have a friend, | T: by anyone wanting a friend.”
an7.36:4.4 | S: you should keep company with that sort.” | T: (none)
an7.36:4.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.43
an9.43:1.1 | S: “Reverend, they speak of the ‘direct witness’. | T: [Ven. Udāyin:] “‘Bodily witness, bodily witness,’ it is said.
an9.43:1.2 | S: What is the direct witness that the Buddha spoke of?” | T: To what extent is one described by the Blessed One as a bodily witness?”
an9.43:2.1 | S: “First, take a mendicant who, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … enters and remains in the first absorption. | T: [Ven. Ānanda:] “There is the case, my friend, where a monk, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an9.43:2.2 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. | T: He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there.
an9.43:2.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the direct witness in a qualified sense. | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as a bodily witness, though with a sequel.
an9.43:3.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, enters and remains in the second absorption … | T: “And further, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna…
an9.43:3.2 | S: third absorption … | T: the third jhāna…
an9.43:3.3 | S: fourth absorption. | T: the fourth jhāna…
an9.43:3.4 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. | T: (none)
an9.43:3.5 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the direct witness in a qualified sense. | T: (none)
an9.43:4.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite space. | T: the dimension of the infinitude of space…
an9.43:4.2 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. | T: (none)

