an9.43:4.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the direct witness in a qualified sense. Furthermore, take a mendicant who enters and remains in the dimension of infinite consciousness … the dimension of nothingness … the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception … | T: the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there. It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as a bodily witness, though with a sequel.
an9.43:5.1 | S: Furthermore, take a mendicant who, going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, enters and remains in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “Then, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, he enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling. And as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended.
an9.43:5.2 | S: They meditate directly experiencing that dimension in every way. | T: He remains touching with his body in whatever way there is an opening there.
an9.43:5.3 | S: To this extent the Buddha spoke of the direct witness in a definitive sense.” | T: It’s to this extent that one is described by the Blessed One as a bodily witness without a sequel.”
an9.43:5.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti108
iti108:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti108:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, those mendicants who are deceivers and flatterers, pompous and fake, insolent and scattered: those mendicants are no followers of mine. | T: “Monks, any monks who are deceitful, stubborn, talkers, frauds, arrogant, & uncentered are not followers of mine.
iti108:2.2 | S: They’ve left this teaching and training, and they don’t achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training. | T: They have turned away from this Dhamma-&-Vinaya. They attain, in terms of this Dhamma-&-Vinaya, no growth, increase, or abundance.
iti108:2.3 | S: But those mendicants who are genuine, not flatterers, attentive, amenable, and serene: those mendicants are followers of mine. | T: “But any monks who are not deceitful, not talkers, who are enlightened, pliant, & well-centered: they are followers of mine.
iti108:2.4 | S: They haven’t left this teaching and training, and they achieve growth, improvement, or maturity in this teaching and training.” | T: They have not turned away from this Dhamma-&-Vinaya. They attain, in terms of this Dhamma-&-Vinaya, growth, increase, & abundance.
iti108:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti108:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti108:3.1 | S: “Those who are deceivers and flatterers, <j>pompous and fake, | T: Deceitful, stubborn, talkers, frauds,
iti108:3.2 | S: insolent and scattered: | T: arrogant, uncentered:
iti108:3.3 | S: these don’t grow in the teaching | T: they don’t grow in the Dhamma
iti108:3.4 | S: that was taught by the perfected Buddha. | T: taught by the Rightly Self-awakened One.
iti108:4.1 | S: But those who are genuine, not flatterers, attentive, | T: Not deceitful, not talkers, enlightened, pliant,
iti108:4.2 | S: amenable, and serene: | T: well-centered:
iti108:4.3 | S: these do grow in the teaching | T: they grow in the Dhamma
iti108:4.4 | S: that was taught by the perfected Buddha.” | T: taught by the One Rightly Self-awakened.
iti108:5.1 | S: This too is a matter that was spoken by the Blessed One: that is what I heard. | T: (none)
iti108:5.2 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## iti52
iti52:1.1 | S: This was said by the Buddha, the Perfected One: that is what I heard. | T: This was said by the Blessed One, said by the Arahant, so I have heard:
iti52:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these three feelings. | T: “Monks, there are these three feelings.
iti52:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
iti52:2.3 | S: Pleasant, painful, and neutral feeling. | T: A feeling of pleasure, a feeling of pain, a feeling of neither pleasure nor pain.
iti52:2.4 | S: These are the three feelings.” | T: These are the three feelings.”
iti52:2.5 | S: The Buddha spoke this matter. | T: (none)
iti52:2.6 | S: On this it is said: | T: (none)
iti52:3.1 | S: “Stilled, aware, | T: Centered, alert,
